<div class="qul-docs">
  <h2>About root data</h2>

  <p>
    Most Arabic words are built from a <strong>root</strong>: a few consonants that carry a core meaning.
    Different patterns of vowels and extra letters around those consonants make different, related words.
    Most roots have three letters; a few have four.
  </p>

  <p>
    This resource gives the root of every word that has one, so you can group words by meaning and find every
    word in the Quran that comes from the same root.
  </p>

  <h3>One root, many words</h3>
  <p>
    The root <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ر ح م</span> (<code>rHm</code>) is about
    mercy. It appears 339 times in the Quran, in words such as:
  </p>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="[&_td]:align-middle">
      <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Word</th>
        <th>Location</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
        <th>Words from this lemma</th>
      </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="me-quran docs-arabic" dir="rtl">ٱلرَّحِيمِ</span></td>
        <td><code>1:3:2</code></td>
        <td>the Especially Merciful</td>
        <td>116</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="me-quran docs-arabic" dir="rtl">رَحۡمَةٗ</span></td>
        <td><code>44:6:1</code></td>
        <td>mercy</td>
        <td>114</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="me-quran docs-arabic" dir="rtl">ٱلرَّحۡمَٰنِ</span></td>
        <td><code>1:3:1</code></td>
        <td>the Most Merciful</td>
        <td>57</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="me-quran docs-arabic" dir="rtl">رَحِمۡنَٰهُمۡ</span></td>
        <td><code>23:75:2</code></td>
        <td>We had mercy on them</td>
        <td>28</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="me-quran docs-arabic" dir="rtl">أَرۡحَامُكُمۡ</span></td>
        <td><code>60:3:3</code></td>
        <td>your relatives (literally "wombs")</td>
        <td>12</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="me-quran docs-arabic" dir="rtl">بِٱلۡمَرۡحَمَةِ</span></td>
        <td><code>90:17:9</code></td>
        <td>to compassion</td>
        <td>1</td>
      </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Words without a root</h3>
  <p>
    About 50,300 of the Quran's words have a root. Particles such as
    <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">مِنَ</span> ("from", <code>2:22:9</code>), pronouns and
    demonstratives such as <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ذَٰلِكَ</span> ("that",
    <code>2:2:1</code>) are not built from a root, so they have no row in this data.
  </p>

  <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-tip">
    In the Preview tab, click a root under any word to see the other words that come from it.
  </div>

  <h2>SQLite format</h2>
  <p>
    The download is a SQLite database with two tables: <code>roots</code> (1,642 rows, one per root) and
    <code>root_words</code> (one row per word that has a root).
  </p>

  <h3>Table: <code>roots</code></h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Column</th>
      <th>Type</th>
      <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>id</code></td>
      <td>INTEGER</td>
      <td>Root id, used by <code>root_words.root_id</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>arabic_trilateral</code></td>
      <td>TEXT</td>
      <td>Root letters in Arabic, separated by spaces</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>english_trilateral</code></td>
      <td>TEXT</td>
      <td>The same letters in Latin script, one letter each (see below)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>words_count</code></td>
      <td>INTEGER</td>
      <td>How many words in the Quran come from this root</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>uniq_words_count</code></td>
      <td>INTEGER</td>
      <td>How many different word forms those words take</td>
    </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="docs-code">
    <div class="docs-code-header"><span>Sample rows</span></div>
    <pre><code>id   | arabic_trilateral | english_trilateral | words_count | uniq_words_count
1356 | ر ح م             | rHm                | 339         | 47
450  | ح م د             | Hmd                | 68          | 13
1572 | ع ل م             | Elm                | 854         | 106
686  | ك و ك ب           | kwkb               | 5           | 3</code></pre>
  </div>

  <h3>Reading <code>english_trilateral</code></h3>
  <p>
    Each Arabic letter is written as exactly one Latin character, using the Buckwalter transliteration.
    Upper and lower case are different letters: <code>H</code> is <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ح</span>
    and <code>h</code> is <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ه</span>.
  </p>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="[&_td]:align-middle [&_td]:text-center [&_th]:text-center">
      <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>A</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ا</span></td>
        <td><code>b</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ب</span></td>
        <td><code>t</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ت</span></td>
        <td><code>v</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ث</span></td>
        <td><code>j</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ج</span></td>
        <td><code>H</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ح</span></td>
        <td><code>x</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">خ</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>d</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">د</span></td>
        <td><code>*</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ذ</span></td>
        <td><code>r</code> <span class="me-quran docs-arabic-inline" dir="rtl">ر</span></td>
